] [17592186268722 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b1b80840-f020-56db-8303-eb251d19e950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269139 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269139 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137096" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269139 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269139 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a-\\frac{3b}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269139 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7264a872-6f66-52f9-bc5f-7b2565f54aa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269139 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 \\\\ 18 & 36\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 18 & 36 & -18\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 18 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-1-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-\\frac{3b}{c}=-1-\\frac{3(-2)}{1}=5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323994 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323994 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323994 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "027310f9-ee0d-507a-bc28-d9da862bc3bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324034 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324034 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324034 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06c8cf07-1252-5e91-b5c9-12f38c1f946f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324150 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324150 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324150 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13cbf72a-775b-5500-b4ce-fd7dee8abc45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324278 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324278 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324278 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2563641c-3674-599a-bc9b-6a7ff8dc6db8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324383 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324383 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324383 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3380e44e-eb6f-5319-9afd-04efe1186590" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324458 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324458 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/re