25674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326532 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326532 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326532 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186325479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326532 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ac5ff18b-ee0a-5c8e-a1ac-a863dbac8c0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326897 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326897 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326897 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326897 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a-3b-6c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326897 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db567127-1e8b-50cb-a3d2-dc5e2ea462cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326897 #edb/ref 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 & 0 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(1/6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(1/2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable.  \n\nFrom the third row, we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{3}=1,\n$$\n\nand from the first row we obtain\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a-3b-6c=2(\\frac{1}{2})-3(1)-6(0)=-2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326913 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326913 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41904" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326913 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326913 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $15a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "85d7c223-d931-53ec-98fb-80a06e5b2764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326913 #edb/ref 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}40 & -20 \\\\ -20 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ -10 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n15a+\\frac{b}{c}=15\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1}=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367437 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367437 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367437 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367437 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186367440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367437 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ac10a081-d7a4-5b23-9a50-e6500fcd48c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367438 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367438 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367438 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c464ae52-c792-5966-9d49-a2e4829b9b98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367439 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367439 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367439 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "60181620-1848-5691-9b01-0836bdae2569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367440 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367440 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367440 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ffce98a-3785-54ef-a67a-a79256e5e528" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367441 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367441 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367441 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "61d68627-501b-5d8d-a22c-84fdf7f0c3a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367442 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367442 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367442 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5a336a5d-1d0b-5110-8865-6d48c421f50f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367443 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186367444 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367443 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137090" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367443 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367443 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-4a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367443 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9b18fd2-40c6-532b-a289-0305c371077f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367443 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & -9 \\\\ -18 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}54 & -18 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{9}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-4a+\\frac{b}{c}=-4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6})+\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367444 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367444 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367444 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367444 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367444 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186367449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367444 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367444 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367444 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186367447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367444 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "778fca66-9171-578b-a82f-9dd5127d9503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367445 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367445 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367445 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "faba9b8c-2db9-5102-aa65-00748558a463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367446 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367446 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367446 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e2d0ce5d-e84e-57e4-8a47-bf9cb06a4a06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367447 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367447 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186367447 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ec1f8972-dbc3-5adb-b2ee-427452d50a48" #edb/ref 17592186045417